 "seo_title": "Year 8 Expanding and Factorising Worksheets: Linear Expressions and the Distributive Law (Free, Answer Key)",
 "short_title": "Expanding and factorising",
 "domain": "Algebra",
 "standard_code": "AC9M8A01",
 "standard_url": "https://v9.australiancurriculum.edu.au/f-10-curriculum/learning-areas/mathematics",
 "standard_text": "create, expand, factorise, rearrange and simplify linear expressions, applying the associative, commutative, identity, distributive and inverse properties",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 8 maths worksheets on expanding and factorising for the Australian Curriculum (AC9M8A01): expanding brackets, expanding and simplifying, factorising with the highest common factor and the laws of arithmetic. 100 questions with an answer key.",
 "key_big": "Expand: multiply every term inside the bracket. Factorise: take out the highest common factor.",
 "key_sub": "Expanding and factorising are opposites, so you can check one with the other.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>To <b>expand</b>, multiply every term inside the bracket by the number outside (the distributive law). Watch the signs when the number outside is negative.</p><p>To <b>factorise</b>, find the highest common factor of the terms and take it outside the bracket. Check by expanding.</p><p>The <b>commutative</b> and <b>associative</b> laws let you reorder and regroup terms; the <b>identity</b> laws (+ 0, × 1) and <b>inverse</b> laws (<i>a</i> − <i>a</i> = 0) help you simplify.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Expand −3(2<i>x</i> − 5).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>−3 × 2<i>x</i> = −6<i>x</i>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>−3 × (−5) = +15.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: −6<i>x</i> + 15</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Expand and simplify 4(<i>x</i> + 2) + 3(<i>x</i> − 1).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>4<i>x</i> + 8 + 3<i>x</i> − 3.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 7<i>x</i> + 5</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Factorise fully 18<i>x</i> − 24.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>The HCF of 18 and 24 is 6.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 6(3<i>x</i> − 4)</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Multiplying only the first term: 3(2<i>x</i> − 5) is not 6<i>x</i> − 5.</li><li>Losing the sign: −2(<i>x</i> − 3) = −2<i>x</i> + 6, not −2<i>x</i> − 6.</li><li>Not factorising fully: 12<i>x</i> + 18 = 6(2<i>x</i> + 3), not 2(6<i>x</i> + 9).</li><li>Combining unlike terms: 3<i>x</i> + 2<i>y</i> cannot be simplified to 5<i>xy</i>.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is the difference between expanding and factorising?",
   "a": "Expanding removes brackets by multiplying out. Factorising puts brackets back in by taking out a common factor. They are opposites."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is the distributive law?",
   "a": "a(b + c) = ab + ac. The number outside the bracket multiplies every term inside."
  },
  {
   "q": "What does factorise fully mean?",
   "a": "Take out the highest common factor, so nothing more can come out of the bracket."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I expand a bracket with a negative outside?",
   "a": "Multiply each term by the negative number. A negative times a negative is positive: −2(x − 3) = −2x + 6."
  }
 ],
